(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609108500-the-whole-component-port-is-generated-from-the-oracles-own-table")
  (:adr/topic "whole-component-port-is-generated")
  (:adr/title "The whole-component port is generated from the oracle's own table, and the check that matters is the refusal")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang" "kotoba-lang/amu"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609081400-a-grammar-is-a-surface-not-a-language"
                "adr-2609106000-the-kir-block-shape-is-an-ipld-schema-and-what-it-cannot-pin"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

`lang/q9-migration.edn` names
`:requalify-wave-1-pilots-as-whole-component-builds` as the next action. On the
morning of 2026-09-10 two of the twenty wave-1 pilots had a whole-component
build. The other eighteen carried `page_limit.kotoba` / `paginate.kotoba`
slices, which ADR-q9-whole-component-build-migration does not count as
migrations.

The twenty oracles are one generated actor template. Diffing
`src/aave/main.cljc` against `src/abb_robotics/main.cljc` with the repository
name normalised away leaves the docstring and the `entity-specs` table, and
nothing else.

## Decision 1: port by generation, because the hand port failed in a way hand
ports will keep failing

The third pilot was ported by hand and carried com-aadhaar's
`{\"expand\":\"identityId\"}` into the expand oracle. The new ref entity has no
`identityId`. Left as it was, every selector would have named a field that does
not exist, `expand` would have folded over nothing, and four arms would have
gone green -- in the one port whose entire novelty was that the fold now runs
twice.

It was caught by reading, not by any check. That is the argument for a
generator: `kotoba-lang/scripts/gen-whole-component.cljs` derives every entity
name, plural, id prefix, field list, required list, coercion pair and ref pair
from the oracle's own table, so the class cannot be carried.

It is VALIDATED against the port that was executed: its tables for
com-abb-robotics come out byte-identical to the hand-written ones that were run
and measured on aarch64.

## Decision 2: the load-bearing check is the refusal, not the compile

`amu check` passes on a component whose tables still answer the TEMPLATE's id
prefixes. That is measured, not hypothetical -- the generator's first run
produced exactly that, and it compiled.

Two things had to be learned to make the refusal real, and both are carried in
the file so the next reader does not re-learn them:

**Order.** Inserting the generated tables first and then deleting the seven
originals by name deletes the GENERATED ones: the block finder takes the first
match, and after the insert that is the new text. The originals survive, the
token pass rewrites their entity names, and the result compiles while
`id-prefix-of` answers the template's prefixes. Delete first, insert second.

**A check written from the substitution table can only see what the
substitution table already handles.** The first evidence floor compared against
the canon map. The four tokens that actually survived were `abbrobot_cha`,
`abbrobot_wor`, `abbrobot_rob` and `abbrobot_pro` -- id prefixes of template
entities the canon map never names. The check now looks for the template
repository's stems, and it was verified to REFUSE with exit 2 on exactly the
broken ordering that had got through.

## Decision 3: the per-repository record says what that repository does NOT
exercise

Every pilot was executed, not merely built, and the expand oracle is the same
six selectors everywhere. Reading them as if they meant the same thing in every
repository would be false in two directions:

- Where the ref entity carries ONE ref, selector 4 (all refs) equals selector 0
  (the one ref). That is correct, and it is the difference from
  com-abb-robotics, whose BOM carries two and whose selectors differ.
- Where NO spec carries refs at all -- com-aftership and com-adyen, both of
  which also have five entities rather than six -- five of the six selectors
  collapse and the sixth differs only because it reads a different ROW. The
  fold is UNEXERCISED there and the record says so.

The batch lander was branching on none of this until com-aftership made it
visible; before that it would have written `selector 0 expands a ref and
differs` into a record where nothing was expanded. A boilerplate reading over a
measured value is a sentence the record cannot support.

## What this does not earn

- No consumer cutover anywhere. Every `main.cljc` remains the oracle and
  nothing imports a migrated component.
- No value-by-value parity harness against the CLJC oracle. The `oracle-*`
  functions were RUN and their values discriminate, but they were not compared
  against the oracle's own answers.
- No wasm32 build. Only `aarch64-macos` was compiled and executed.
- The tranche status is unchanged. More whole-component builds do not earn the
  soak, which wants green fleet receipts and calendar days.
- The generator is written in nbb. CLAUDE.md puts new operational tooling on
  kbb first; measured on this machine, `which kbb` finds nothing. The reason is
  recorded in the file rather than silently choosing the old host, and the kbb
  port is owed.

## Reproduce

Generation, from the superproject root, against a worktree at origin/main:

    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/<repo> worktree add --detach <dir> origin/main
    nbb orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang/scripts/gen-whole-component.cljs \\
        <template.kotoba> <dir> <src-dir-name>

The template is com-abb-robotics' landed `whole_component.kotoba`. Exit 2 with
`template tokens survived the substitution` is the refusal; to see it fire,
swap the delete and insert steps so the generated tables are inserted first.

Execution, which is what makes a build an acceptance:

    cc -O2 -o kexe-loader orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/tools/kexe_loader.c
    amu compile <dir>/src/<n>/whole_component.kotoba --target aarch64-macos \\
        --policy <dir>/policy.edn --output <dir>/a.kexe
    amu extract-native <dir>/a.kexe --symbol main --output <dir>/main.bin
    KEXE_FUEL=100000000 ./kexe-loader <dir>/main.bin <offset> 0 aarch64 7,23

`main` answers route-count -- five routes per entity -- so it is 30 for a
six-entity oracle and 25 for a five-entity one, computed from the table rather
than written down. Every path must be ABSOLUTE: a relative one is refused with
`:decode` / `input could not be read`, which reads like a malformed source
rather than a path problem.")))
